WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.235
Lesson 2.6.

00:00:02.235 --> 00:00:06.477
Advanced execution: limit, TWAP and intent-based orders.

00:00:06.477 --> 00:00:08.501
By the end of this lesson, you'll be

00:00:08.501 --> 00:00:10.580
able to use order types that give you

00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:13.152
better prices and protection than a plain swap.

00:00:14.452 --> 00:00:15.742
Here's the plan.

00:00:15.742 --> 00:00:17.427
First, the key ideas.

00:00:17.427 --> 00:00:20.142
Then, a worked example, step by step.

00:00:20.142 --> 00:00:21.667
Then, your checklist.

00:00:21.667 --> 00:00:23.829
And finally, a three-question quiz.

00:00:25.129 --> 00:00:28.850
First, On-chain limit orders: you sign an order

00:00:28.850 --> 00:00:32.338
off-chain ("sell 1 ETH at 3,300 or better");

00:00:32.338 --> 00:00:35.584
it fills only if the price is reached.

00:00:35.584 --> 00:00:37.659
Usually gasless until filled.

00:00:37.659 --> 00:00:40.971
Next, TWAP orders: split a large trade into

00:00:40.971 --> 00:00:44.734
equal slices over time to reduce price impact.

00:00:44.734 --> 00:00:48.748
Then, Intent-based / RFQ trading: you state what

00:00:48.748 --> 00:00:52.025
you want ("100,000 USDC → ETH, at least

00:00:52.025 --> 00:00:56.039
X"); competing solvers or market makers fill it,

00:00:56.039 --> 00:01:00.927
often with MEV protection and no failed-transaction gas.

00:01:00.927 --> 00:01:04.550
And finally, Trade-offs: limit orders may never fill;

00:01:04.550 --> 00:01:07.166
TWAP takes time and the price can move

00:01:07.166 --> 00:01:10.587
away; intents rely on the protocol's solver design.

00:01:11.887 --> 00:01:14.402
Let's work an example, step by step.

00:01:14.402 --> 00:01:19.352
Buying $50,000 of ETH in a pool where a

00:01:19.352 --> 00:01:26.087
single $50,000 trade has ~1.5% price impact (~$750).

00:01:26.087 --> 00:01:28.564
A TWAP of 10 × $5,000 over an hour

00:01:28.564 --> 00:01:31.537
has far less impact per slice, as long as

00:01:31.537 --> 00:01:35.517
the price doesn't trend against you during the hour.

00:01:35.517 --> 00:01:37.945
An intent-based order may beat both by

00:01:37.945 --> 00:01:41.172
sourcing liquidity from several venues at once.

00:01:41.172 --> 00:01:42.222
Compare the quotes.

00:01:43.522 --> 00:01:44.982
Here's your checklist.

00:01:44.982 --> 00:01:46.517
Do it now, for real.

00:01:46.517 --> 00:01:48.865
First, I compare a plain swap, a TWAP

00:01:48.865 --> 00:01:51.452
and an intent quote for large trades.

00:01:51.452 --> 00:01:54.712
Next, Limit orders have an expiry I've chosen.

00:01:54.712 --> 00:01:56.598
And finally, I know which

00:01:56.598 --> 00:01:59.137
order types include MEV protection.

00:02:00.437 --> 00:02:01.647
Question 1.

00:02:01.647 --> 00:02:03.812
Why split a large trade into a TWAP?

00:02:08.232 --> 00:02:11.816
The answer: Smaller slices cause less price impact each.

00:02:13.116 --> 00:02:14.361
Question 2.

00:02:14.361 --> 00:02:16.056
What's the risk of a limit order?

00:02:20.476 --> 00:02:22.201
The answer: It may never fill.

00:02:23.501 --> 00:02:24.771
Question 3.

00:02:24.771 --> 00:02:26.556
Who fills an intent-based order?

00:02:30.976 --> 00:02:33.916
The answer: Competing solvers or market makers.

00:02:35.216 --> 00:02:36.461
Let's recap.

00:02:36.461 --> 00:02:40.667
On-chain limit orders: you sign an order off-chain

00:02:40.667 --> 00:02:43.771
("sell 1 ETH at 3,300 or better").

00:02:43.771 --> 00:02:47.023
TWAP orders: split a large trade into equal

00:02:47.023 --> 00:02:50.279
slices over time to reduce price impact.

00:02:50.279 --> 00:02:54.539
Intent-based / RFQ trading: you state what you

00:02:54.539 --> 00:02:58.464
want ("100,000 USDC → ETH, at least X").

00:02:58.464 --> 00:03:00.839
Trade-offs: limit orders may never fill.

00:03:02.139 --> 00:03:04.654
Do the checklist now, before you move on.

00:03:04.654 --> 00:03:09.789
Next up, Lesson 2.7: Advanced AMM design and LVR.
